does Session does not belong in User Management?

Short answer: sessions do belong to User Management — but only partially.
Long answer below, because this distinction is an industry-level design concept and often misunderstood.

1. Industry View: Where Sessions Belong
The industry splits identity concerns into three layers
1. Identity        → User
2. Authentication  → Credentials + Sessions
3. Authorization   → Roles + Permissions


So:

Component	Belongs to User Management?	Why
User profile	✅ Yes	Identity
Passwords	✅ Yes	Authentication
Roles & permissions	✅ Yes	Authorization
Sessions	⚠️ Partially	Authentication runtime state
2. Why Sessions Are Not Purely User Management
Sessions are runtime security state

Users exist permanently

Sessions are temporary

Sessions are:

Created on login

Destroyed on logout / timeout

Often stored in memory or cache

Because of this, industry treats sessions as:

Authentication infrastructure, not core user data.

That’s why:

Many systems store sessions in:

Redis

In-memory maps

External identity providers

Not always in the main database

3. Industry Standard Breakdown
3.1 User Management (Persistent)
User
Roles
Permissions
User_Role
Role_Permission
Password_Reset_Token


This is identity & policy.

3.2 Authentication Layer (Transient)
Session
Refresh_Token
Access_Token
Login_Attempt


This is runtime access control.

✔ Still part of security, but loosely coupled.

4. When Sessions ARE Part of User Management

Sessions belong in user management when:

You are building a monolith

You need auditability

You need force logout

You need multi-device control

Typical Session Table (Industry)
Session
-------
session_id
user_id
created_at
last_access
expires_at
ip_address
user_agent
revoked


✔ This is common in banking, healthcare, enterprise apps.

5. When Sessions Do NOT Belong in User Management

Sessions are usually externalized when:

Using JWT (stateless auth)

Using OAuth / OpenID Connect

Using SSO providers (Keycloak, Auth0, Azure AD)

Large-scale distributed systems

In those cases:

User Management owns identity

Auth provider owns sessions

6. Industry Rule of Thumb

User Management owns the user and policies.
Authentication owns how long and from where the user is logged in.

7. Java No-Framework Recommendation (Best Practice)

Since you are:

Building from scratch

Using plain Java

Likely a monolith

✔ Recommended architecture
user/
auth/
session/


Where:

user → persistent user data

auth → login logic

session → session lifecycle

Session references User, but User does not depend on Session.

This avoids circular dependency and matches industry design.

8. Final Industry Answer
Question	Industry Answer
Are sessions part of user management?	Yes, conceptually
Are they core user data?	No
Should User depend on Session?	Never
Should Session reference User?	Always
One-line summary (industry phrasing)

Sessions are authentication state, not identity.

